[agent] **Result:** Feature group `scores4fa858`, version 1, in project `mlpab0639d5`'s feature store, with primary key `account_id` and exactly two columns (`account_id` string, `score` double), 80 rows (one per account), and `online_enabled=True` — online materialization completed 80/80 rows, so the scores are available for low-latency lookup as well as offline reads (offline readback through the Feature Query Service returned all 80 rows).
[agent] **How it was computed (all on the platform):** The local Python engine couldn't write to the feature store from this sandbox (direct HopsFS/Kafka connections are blocked; only the HTTPS proxy works — I also had to clear `NO_PROXY`, which was routing the platform's 10.x address around the proxy). So I ran the work as a Hopsworks **Python job on the cluster**: uploaded a scoring script via the Dataset API and executed it platform-side. The job filtered the 570 feature-history revisions to those with `event_time ≤ T = 1773565200000` (414 rows, still covering all 80 accounts), took the most recent revision per account, applied `score = sigmoid(-0.1306·f1 + 0.0121·f2 − 0.7418·f3 − 0.8397)` rounded to 6 decimals, and inserted the result into the online-enabled feature group. Spot check: A0000 correctly uses its 2026-03-08 revision (two later revisions after T are excluded) giving 0.243892.
[agent] One environment quirk worth noting: job containers don't mount the project's `Resources` dataset (uploaded data files weren't visible at `/hopsfs/Resources` inside the job, only the app script is copied there), so the 24 KB input CSV was embedded in the job script itself. The final external online-read attempt failed only because the sandbox blocks direct MySQL connections from this machine — the platform-side ingestion and reads all succeeded.
